### Shock cooling with a central engine

In Fig. 4, we show an alternative scenario, comparing the early emission from LSQ14bdq to other SNe with two observed peaks. The basic light curve morphology of SNe 1987A, 1993J and 2008D has been interpreted as an initial cooling phase, releasing heat deposited by the shock wave, before a second peak is driven by delayed heating from {}^{56}Ni (Shigeyama & Nomoto, 1990; Woosley et al., 1994; Modjaz et al., 2009). The light curve of LSQ14bdq is qualitatively similar to these objects, suggesting that

